L000402	\subsection{Shift symmetries and the geometric interpretation}
L000403	
L000404	In a single copy, the tachyonic vector has the same mass (in units of the round $S^{d-1}$ radius) as in the $S^{d+1}$ case, while the two tachyonic scalars of \eqref{eq:Zedgegravresult} are replaced by two massless scalars. This spectrum reflects that $Z_\text{edge}$ again nonlinearly realizes the background isometries: the number of shift symmetries (the $SO(d)$ generators from the vector plus three constant shifts) equals the dimension of the isometry group $SO(3)\times SO(d)$.
L000405	
L000406	In terms of geometric fluctuations of the horizon $S^{d-1}$, the vector has the same mass (in units of $r_N$) as in the round $S^{d+1}$ case and continues to describe diffeomorphisms intrinsic to $S^{d-1}$, while another massless scalar continues to encode the $SO(2)$ normal-bundle connection, i.e. local rotations of the two transverse directions.
L000407	
L000408	The remaining question is whether the two other massless scalars can be viewed as coordinates transverse to the Euclidean horizon. Indeed, embedding a round $S^{d-1}$ into $S^2\times S^{d-1}$ gives the induced metric \eqref{eq:inducedmetric}, but with
L000409	\begin{align}
L000410	    f^2 = 1 \;, \qquad
L000411	    B_{\mu\nu} = \ell_N^2\, \partial_\mu \phi^a \partial_\nu \phi_a + O(\phi^4) \;.
L000412	\end{align}
L000413	The trivial dependence of $f^2$ on $\phi^a$ makes these scalars massless: constant shifts of the $S^{d-1}$ along the $S^2$ factor leaves its radius unchanged.
